#
# Template. entrypoint.sh substitutes @Z_FILTER@ before handing this to the
# node. Algorithm parameters (base, coeff_1, coeff_2, scale_factor) are exactly
# Intel's adbscan_sub_RS.yaml values; subsample_ratio is not, see below. Only
# the input and the geometry differ otherwise, and every difference is recorded.
#
# INPUT: the groundfloor node's output, not the raw depth cloud.
#
# ADBSCAN removes the ground with a single height threshold, z_filter, applied
# in the frame it is given. That works on their AAEON robot, where the camera is
# level and the floor really is one z. This camera is pitched 14.08 deg down,
# and in a camera-centred frame the floor then RAMPS: measured on this room,
#
#   world x = 1.5 m  ->  z = -1.148     world x = 4.5 m  ->  z = -0.418
#   world x = 3.0 m  ->  z = -0.783     world x = 6.5 m  ->  z = +0.068
#
# a 1.22 m span across the arena (x*tan(14.08)). No single threshold can cut
# that: the value that clears the near floor keeps the far floor, and the far
# floor is a large connected sheet of points -- which is precisely the
# 6.14 x 6.22 m room-spanning cluster ADBSCAN reported on the raw cloud.
#
# The suite's own answer is to compose the bricks: groundfloor removes the floor
# with a tilt-aware plane fit and publishes what is left on
# /segmentation/obstacle_points, already in base_link. ADBSCAN then clusters
# points that are known not to be floor.
#
# Lidar_type is 3D, not RS. The RS path rotates every input point by
# (x,y,z) <- (z,-x,-y), the optical-to-body swap, because it expects a raw
# RealSense cloud in the optical frame. groundfloor's output is ALREADY in
# base_link (x forward, y left, z up, origin on the ground under the camera), so
# the RS rotation would scramble it. 3D takes the cloud as given.
#
# That also makes base_link this project's world frame by construction, so the
# cluster positions come back needing no transform at all.
#
#   x_filter_back  3.0 -> 6.5   now measured from the ground origin, so it is
#                               directly the far wall at 6.2 m plus a little.
#   y_filter_*    +-2.0 -> +-4.0  our floor spans -2.6 to +1.5 m across.
#   z_filter      -0.1 -> +tol  KEPT AS A BACKSTOP, not as the ground removal.
#                               FILTER_OP_LESS_THAN drops points BELOW it, and
#                               with the floor already gone and the ground at
#                               z = 0, a small positive value only catches
#                               whatever the plane fit left behind.
---
adbscan_sub_node:
  ros__parameters:
    Lidar_type: 3D
    # Not /segmentation/obstacle_points directly. bridge.py clips that cloud to
    # the arena interior and republishes it here. groundfloor removes the floor
    # but not the walls, and DBSCAN chains through contact: a chair against the
    # wall comes back as one mass with the wall. The uncut cloud is still on
    # /segmentation/obstacle_points for debugging.
    Lidar_topic: /segmentation/arena_points
    # Their Verbose logs every cluster's centre and size on every frame, which
    # at 9 Hz buries the bridge's own reporting. The bridge summarises instead.
    Verbose: False
    # 150 -> 75, the one algorithm parameter that departs from Intel's file.
    # subsample_LiDAR_data (doDBSCAN.cpp:58) is a stride, not a fraction: it
    # keeps one point in `ratio`. Their 150 is sized for a raw RealSense cloud;
    # our input is groundfloor's obstacle_points, which is what is LEFT after
    # the floor is removed -- a small fraction of the cloud 150 was chosen for.
    # 75 therefore doubles the points reaching the clusterer.
    #
    # Measured 75 / 150 / 75 in that order, three 60 s runs on one scene (the
    # order matters, see docs/ETAPE-B-RESULTS.md section 7 -- comparing against a
    # number from another session proves nothing). Raw floor IoU held at
    # 0.646 / 0.658 / 0.657 across the three, which is the control: this
    # parameter cannot reach the groundfloor node, so a stable floor says the
    # scene did not move under the measurement.
    #
    #                            75          150          75
    #   clusters in arena       1.4          1.9         1.6
    #   matched to ours         0.8          0.8         0.9
    #   theirs only             0.6          1.1         0.7
    #   match rate              27%          28%         28%
    #   ADBSCAN stage        ~27 ms        ~7 ms
    #
    # Those three runs predate the arena clip in bridge.py, and the cost column
    # no longer holds: with the walls cut out the ADBSCAN stage at 75 runs at
    # ~2.9 ms, cheaper than 150 ever was uncut. The 4x penalty below was the
    # wall sheet, not the point count. The cluster columns are the reason this
    # stays at 75; see the clip comment on Lidar_topic for what changed after.
    #
    # More points do NOT find more objects. The extra density MERGES clusters:
    # the count drops by a fifth, and the entire drop is in "theirs only" while
    # matched stays flat -- the signature of a spurious cluster being absorbed
    # into a neighbour that was already matched. The match rate does not move.
    #
    # Kept at 75. The cost objection it was kept in spite of -- about 4x in the
    # clustering stage, 7 -> 27 ms -- turned out to be the wall, not the points:
    # once bridge.py clips the arena the same 75 costs ~2.9 ms. What remains
    # true is that 0.6 vs 1.1 "theirs only" could be read as losing real
    # obstacles we never had a footprint for, rather than as noise absorbed.
    subsample_ratio: 75.0
    x_filter_back: 6.5
    y_filter_left: 4.0
    y_filter_right: -4.0
    z_filter: @Z_FILTER@
    Z_based_ground_removal: 1.0
    base: 5.29545454
    coeff_1: -0.164835164
    coeff_2: -0.017982017
    scale_factor: 0.9
    oneapi_library: kdtree
    benchmark_number_of_frames: 1
